From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] windows.ui.xaml: visual states, storyboards, transforms and TabView's list pieces

Windows Terminal's tab row is MUX TabView: a ListView of the program's TabViewItems with templates full of VisualStateGroups, Storyboards and x:Bind setters, animated with render transforms. Visual states are now real: VisualStateManager.GoToState sets a state's Setters and its Storyboard's end values as animated values above the local ones and takes them back for the next state (a program's CustomVisualStateManager decides through GoToStateCore); the managers are composable. Storyboard, DoubleAnimation, ColorAnimation, the key-frame animations and KeySpline are there, a program's own Storyboard begins, completes and stops. Translate, Scale, Rotate, Skew, Composite, Matrix transforms and TransformGroup are GeneralTransforms, and an element's RenderTransform is drawn. The list pieces TabView needs: ListViewBase statics and ListViewBase2..5, Selector's SelectedIndex/SelectedItem properties, IItemContainerMapping, a ListViewItem of the program's as its own container, and a control's DefaultStyleResourceUri (a library's generic.xaml). A Grid measures each child once in a pass and elements keep the results of their last few constraints, so TabView's layout settles (it flip-flopped for ever). A DependencyObject's Dispatcher is that of the thread it was made on (Windows Terminal asks from its render thread). ElementCompositionPreview gives elements' visuals.
